#!/usr/bin/env bash
# PostToolUse guard: a spec refinement written instead of raised.
#
# A model cannot be relied on to notice that the code contradicts the spec --
# only reading both does that. But the SYMPTOM of skipping the interrupt is
# exactly detectable: a plan gains a refinement marker in a state that means
# "someone will deal with this later".
#
#   [OPEN]      legacy. Nothing new is ever written in it.
#   [DEFERRED]  only the user decides to defer. Never assume one for them.
#
# [BLOCKING] is deliberately NOT caught: writing it is the correct move.
#
# Reads the PostToolUse payload on stdin; exit 2 feeds stderr back as feedback.

payload=$(cat)

file=$(printf '%s' "$payload" | jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty')
case "$file" in
  */plans/*|plans/*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

# Edit carries new_string, Write carries content; MultiEdit carries neither, so
# fall back to the whole tool_input rather than missing the marker entirely.
added=$(printf '%s' "$payload" |
  jq -r '.tool_input.new_string // .tool_input.content // (.tool_input | tostring)')

if printf '%s' "$added" |
     grep -qiE 'spec refinement[[:space:]]*\[[[:space:]]*(OPEN|DEFERRED)'; then
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
spec-refinement guard: this edit adds a refinement marker in a deferring state.

A refinement is an interrupt, not a queue entry (.claude/rules/spec-refinement.md):

  [OPEN]      legacy only -- do not write a new one.
  [DEFERRED]  the USER decides to defer, and the reason must be theirs.

If the spec is false or contradicts what the implementation must do: write
[BLOCKING], stop implementing that thread, and ask the user. If the spec is
merely silent, that is a gap the plan may fill -- drop the marker entirely.
EOF
  exit 2
fi

exit 0
